  "grammar_points": [
    {
      "name": "〜ことで",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: ことで (koto de) means 'by doing ~' — it indicates the means or method by which something is achieved. こと (koto) nominalizes the verb, and で (de) marks the means.\n\nExample from this song: The song uses ことで to express how something is achieved — 'by (doing this action), (this result happens).'\n\nMore examples:\n• 練習することで上手になる (renshuu suru koto de jouzu ni naru, 'by practicing, you get better')\n• 話すことで気持ちが楽になる (hanasu koto de kimochi ga raku ni naru, 'by talking, you feel better')\n• 読むことで世界が広がる (yomu koto de sekai ga hirogaru, 'by reading, your world expands')\n\nNote: こと turns a verb into a noun (nominalizer). Adding で means 'by means of that action.' This is useful for explaining cause-and-effect or methods."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "verb dictionary form + ことで (koto de, 'by doing')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜さえ(も)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: さえ (sae) means 'even,' emphasizing an extreme or unexpected case. Adding も (mo) intensifies it further. It implies 'if even this extreme thing is true, then everything else surely is too.'\n\nExample from this song: The song uses さえ to emphasize something surprising — 'even (this thing you wouldn't expect)...'\n\nMore examples:\n• 名前さえ知らない (namae sae shiranai, 'don't even know the name') — most basic info is unknown\n• 子供さえもできる (kodomo sae mo dekiru, 'even children can do it') — emphasizing ease\n• 立つことさえできない (tatsu koto sae dekinai, 'can't even stand') — extreme inability\n\nNote: さえ replaces が or を. さえも is more emphatic than さえ alone. Pattern: noun + さえ or verb te-form + さえ. It creates dramatic emphasis."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "noun + さえ (sae, 'even') / さえも (sae mo, 'even')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜てくれる",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: くれる (kureru) after a verb's te-form indicates someone does something for the speaker's benefit. It expresses gratitude.\n\nExample from this song: The song uses てくれる to acknowledge someone doing something beneficial — there's warmth and thankfulness in this expression.\n\nMore examples:\n• 教えてくれた (oshiete kureta, 'taught me') — from 教える (oshieru, 'to teach')\n• 信じてくれる (shinjite kureru, 'believes in me') — from 信じる (shinjiru, 'to believe')\n• 来てくれてありがとう (kite kurete arigatou, 'thanks for coming') — from 来る (kuru, 'to come')\n\nNote: てくれる is uniquely Japanese — it marks that an action was received as a favor. Without it, the sentence is neutral. With it, gratitude is built into the grammar itself."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "verb te-form + くれる (kureru, 'does for me')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜なけりゃ (casual conditional)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: なけりゃ (nakerya) is the casual spoken contraction of なければ (nakereba), meaning 'if not.' It's common in songs, anime, and informal speech.\n\nExample from this song: The song uses なけりゃ as a casual 'if not' — expressing what needs to happen or what would go wrong without something.\n\nMore examples:\n• 行かなけりゃ (ikanakerya, 'if I don't go') — from 行く (iku, 'to go')\n• やらなけりゃダメだ (yaranakerya dame da, 'you gotta do it') — from やる (yaru, 'to do')\n• 食べなけりゃ死ぬ (tabenakerya shinu, 'if you don't eat, you'll die')\n\nNote: Contraction chain: なければ → なけりゃ → なきゃ. なきゃ is even more casual. All three mean 'if not' / 'must.' なけりゃならない = 'must do' (casual of なければならない)."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "verb negative stem + なければ (nakereba, 'if not') → なけりゃ (nakerya, casual) → なきゃ (nakya, ultra-casual)"
    },
    {
      "name": "volitional form 〜よう/〜おう",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: The volitional form expresses 'let's do' or 'I shall do.' For godan verbs, change -u to -ou. For ichidan verbs, replace る with よう.\n\nExample from this song: 進んでいこう (susunde ikou) = 'let's move forward' — from 進む (susumu, 'to advance') + ていく (te iku) → volitional: いこう (ikou)\n\nMore examples:\n• 食べよう (tabeyou, 'let's eat') — from 食べる (taberu, ichidan)\n• 頑張ろう (ganbarou, 'let's do our best') — from 頑張る (ganbaru, godan)\n• 始めよう (hajimeyou, 'let's begin') — from 始める (hajimeru, ichidan)\n\nNote: The volitional can express suggestion ('let's') or personal resolve ('I shall'). In songs, it often expresses determination: 進んでいこう conveys 'let's keep moving forward together!'"
      },
      "conjugation_path": "進む (susumu, 'to advance') → 進んで (susunde) + いく (iku) → いこう (ikou, volitional) → 進んでいこう (susunde ikou, 'let's move forward')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜ことさえも",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: ことさえも (koto sae mo) combines the nominalizer こと (koto) with the emphatic particle さえも (sae mo, 'even'). It means 'even the act of doing X.'\n\nExample from this song: The song uses ことさえも to emphasize that even the action itself is noteworthy or surprising.\n\nMore examples:\n• 歩くことさえも辛い (aruku koto sae mo tsurai, 'even just walking is painful')\n• 笑うことさえも忘れた (warau koto sae mo wasureta, 'even forgot how to laugh')\n• 生きることさえも怖い (ikiru koto sae mo kowai, 'even living itself is scary')\n\nNote: こと turns the verb into a noun ('the act of doing'). さえも adds 'even.' Together: 'even the very act of X.' This creates strong dramatic emphasis — if even this fundamental action is affected, the situation is extreme."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "verb dictionary form + こと (koto, 'the act of') + さえも (sae mo, 'even') → ことさえも (koto sae mo, 'even the act of')"
    }
  ]
